WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.850
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Hey, Martin.

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.185
Have you lost something?

00:00:03.185 --> 00:00:04.310
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Katie.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:05.870
I'm trying to find my wallet.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.610
I forgot to pay
my internet bill,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.710
and I need to find
my credit card.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.320
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well, why
not set up automatic payments?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.600
I was just in a meeting
with the team from WebNet,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.133
and we helped them set up
their nightly billing job.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.800
Stay tuned if you
want to find out more.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.251
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.930
WebNet is a pay-as-you-go
internet provider where you can

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.720
set up automatic payments
through their app.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.520
They built a nightly
billing job which

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.800
charges the credit
card of any user who

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.175
has built up a significant
balance during the day.

00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:42.550
MARTIN OMANDER:
Their billing job

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.610
runs in Cloud Run,
which is serverless.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.490
This means they don't have
to manage servers or pay

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.960
for idle capacity.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.095
Also, it scales up
automatically as load increases.

00:00:53.095 --> 00:00:54.970
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: They
use Cloud "Shed-juler"

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.240
to kick off the
nightly billing job.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.790
MARTIN OMANDER: Don't
you mean Cloud Scheduler?

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.030
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Nah, I
meant Cloud "Shed-juler."

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.707
Cloud Scheduler is
fully managed by Google,

00:01:06.707 --> 00:01:08.290
which means that the
team doesn't have

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:09.670
to worry about maintenance.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.080
It just works.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.270
MARTIN OMANDER: Cloud
Scheduler triggers code

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.920
by either making an HTTPS call,
or by publishing a Pub/Sub

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:18.850
message.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.440
The team chooses HTTPS.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.440
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: They
configure Cloud Scheduler

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.870
to make a POST request
to their billing service

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.360
nightly at 1:00 AM GMT.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.060
They also set a JSON
payload that specifies

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.350
the minimum balance.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.920
Only customers with a balance
of $5 or more will be billed.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.590
MARTIN OMANDER: Cloud Run lets
you run code in any programming

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.470
language you want, as long
as it's a web service that

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.390
can be containerized.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.400
The team has experience
with JavaScript,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.560
so they pick Node.js.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.200
At the top of their code,
they create and initialize

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.790
an express app that listens
for incoming HTTP calls.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.850
Note that Cloud Scheduler
uses text/plain content-type,

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.210
so they have to tell
bodyParser to parse

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.818
more than just the default
application JSON type.

00:02:04.818 --> 00:02:06.235
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
The post handler

00:02:06.235 --> 00:02:09.280
parses the minimum balance
that was sent to it from Cloud

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.320
Scheduler, and
then sends that off

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.570
to the billing method
later in their code.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.450
MARTIN OMANDER: The billing job
will now be triggered nightly.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.000
But this billing service
is publicly exposed

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.610
to the internet, meaning
anyone can trigger it

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.530
by posting to the URL.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.610
We need to make
it private so only

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.743
Cloud Scheduler can trigger it.

00:02:27.743 --> 00:02:30.160
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: To do that,
the team edits the Cloud Run

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.850
service, removing the allUsers
member from the service, which

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.215
makes it private.

00:02:35.215 --> 00:02:37.700
MARTIN OMANDER: Next, they
create a new service account

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.527
they call Billing Initiator.

00:02:39.527 --> 00:02:41.860
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: They then
give the new service account

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.020
the "Cloud Run Invoker" role.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.150
This means that the
Billing Initiator service

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.670
account can trigger Cloud Run
services, but do nothing else.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.570
MARTIN OMANDER: Next,
they copy the email

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.320
of the Billing Initiator
service account--

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.945
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
And then they open up

00:02:54.945 --> 00:02:57.730
the job in Cloud Scheduler and
under the Auth header setting,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.280
select "Add OIDC token."

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.540
OIDC, or Open ID Connect, is
a small layer on top of OAuth

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.220
that handles identity.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.070
Using OIDC, they can
tell Cloud Scheduler

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.410
to use the Billing
Initiator service account

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.505
as the identity for the job.

00:03:13.505 --> 00:03:15.130
MARTIN OMANDER: Now
the billing service

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.410
is closed to the
public, but it can still

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.600
be triggered by Cloud Scheduler.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.920
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: So the team at
WebNet gave me this US $1 bill.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.860
How much Cloud Scheduler
could they get for that $1?

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.410
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, you get
the three Cloud Scheduler jobs

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.430
for free.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.800
If they've used those
three jobs already,

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.730
they would have to pay $0.10
per month for this job,

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.430
regardless of how many times
the job executes in a month.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:45.160
So that $1 bill would pay for
10 months of Cloud Scheduler.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.220
They would, of course, also have
to pay for Cloud Run, execution

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.222
time, and database storage.

00:03:50.222 --> 00:03:51.680
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
That makes sense.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.840
So what about the--

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.350
Martin?

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:55.360
Martin?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.600
Oh, I guess he didn't
pay his bill in time.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:03:59.800
Oops.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

